      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the probability that exactly two of the five 12-sided dice show an even number, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Determine the probability of rolling an even number on a single die:**\n   Each 12-sided die has the numbers 1 through 12. The even numbers in this range are 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, and 12. There are 6 even numbers out of 12 possible outcomes.\n   Therefore, the probability of rolling an even number on a single die is:\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{even number}) = \\frac{6}{12} = \\frac{1}{2}\n   \\]\n   Similarly, the probability of rolling an odd number on a single die is:\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{odd number}) = \\frac{6}{12} = \\frac{1}{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the number of ways to choose which 2 out of the 5 dice show an even number:**\n   We need to choose 2 dice out of 5 to show an even number. This can be done using the binomial coefficient:\n   \\[\n   \\binom{5}{2} = \\frac{5!}{2!(5-2)!} = \\frac{5!}{2!3!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4}{2 \\times 1} = 10\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the probability of a specific combination of 2 dice showing even numbers and 3 dice showing odd numbers:**\n   The probability of any specific combination where 2 dice show an even number and 3 dice show an odd number is:\n   \\[\n   \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)^2 \\times \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)^3 = \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)^5 = \\frac{1}{32}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the total probability:**\n   The total probability is the number of ways to choose the 2 dice that show even numbers, multiplied by the probability of this specific combination:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total probability} = \\binom{5}{2} \\times \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)^5 = 10 \\times \\frac{1}{32} = \\frac{10}{32} = \\frac{5}{16}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that exactly two of the five dice show an even number is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{5}{16}}\\).",
