      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the probability that exactly two out of five 12-sided dice show an even number, we can use the binomial probability formula. Here's the step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. **Determine the probability of rolling an even number on a single die:**\n   A 12-sided die has the numbers 1 through 12. The even numbers are 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, and 12. There are 6 even numbers out of 12 possible outcomes.\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{even}) = \\frac{6}{12} = \\frac{1}{2}\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the probability of rolling an odd number is also:\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{odd}) = \\frac{1}{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Identify the binomial coefficient:**\n   We need to choose 2 dice out of 5 to show even numbers. The number of ways to choose 2 dice out of 5 is given by the binomial coefficient \\(\\binom{5}{2}\\):\n   \\[\n   \\binom{5}{2} = \\frac{5!}{2!(5-2)!} = \\frac{5!}{2!3!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4}{2 \\times 1} = 10\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the probability of a specific sequence with exactly 2 even numbers:**\n   The probability of a specific sequence where 2 dice show even numbers and 3 dice show odd numbers is:\n   \\[\n   \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2 \\times \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^3 = \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^5 = \\frac{1}{32}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Combine the results using the binomial probability formula:**\n   The total probability is the number of ways to get exactly 2 even numbers times the probability of any specific sequence:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Probability} = \\binom{5}{2} \\times \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^5 = 10 \\times \\frac{1}{32} = \\frac{10}{32} = \\frac{5}{16}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that exactly two of the dice show an even number is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{5}{16}}\n\\]",
